# The firmwares this simulator runs, pinned: a release, and one commit of the
# development branch.
#
# src/seedsigner in the built firmware zip is the tree below, verbatim, with its
# translations compiled in from the commit that tree itself pins for its
# seedsigner-translations submodule. Nothing in this repository patches it: the
# hardware seams are replaced from the outside,
# by the modules in src/shims and by the stand-in packages in src/fakes. That is
# the property the build script exists to let you check for yourself.
#
# A named section, and every consumer of this file names the section it wants:
# build/build-firmware-zip.sh, which assembles the zip, and the two workflows that
# re-derive its hash and run upstream's own tests against the pin.
#
# [stock] is pinned to a release tag rather than a branch tip. A tip moves:
# rebase or force-push and the sha stops existing on the remote, at which point
# every rebuild described here fails at the fetch, for everyone, permanently. A
# tag is a name upstream has published and does not move. The sha is still the
# pin; the tag only says which release it is.
#
# Each [pr-<N>] section is one SeedSigner pull request at the head it had when
# build/pr.sh added or last updated it: somebody's proposed change, unreviewed,
# pinned so it can be tried in the simulator and checked like everything else
# here. build/pr.sh adds, moves and removes them; the page lists them apart and
# says what they are.
#
# [dev] is the exception, on purpose: it is for trying what SeedSigner has merged
# since that release. It is still one sha, not "whatever dev is now", so a build
# of it is as reproducible and as checkable as the release's, and it moves only
# when someone runs build/bump-dev.sh and commits the result. The risk is the one
# above: were dev ever force-pushed past this commit, rebuilding it would fail
# until the pin was moved. Upstream merges into dev by pull request, and that has
# not happened; if it did, the fix is to bump.
#
# The section also publishes the two hashes of the firmware zip that
# build/build-firmware-zip.sh produces from that pin. Rebuild it yourself and
# compare; if these differ, something between that commit and the file you were
# served is not what it claims to be.

# SeedSigner as the SeedSigner project publishes it: the firmware on a plain
# device.
